Problem 3: Palindrome Pairs (Hard – Trie / Hashing)
📌 PROBLEM STATEMENT

You are given an array of unique strings words.

Return all pairs of indices (i, j) such that:

words[i] + words[j]


forms a palindrome.

🧾 Example
Input:
["bat","tab","cat"]

Output:
[[0,1],[1,0]]


Because:

"bat" + "tab" = "battab" ✔ palindrome
"tab" + "bat" = "tabbat" ✔ palindrome

🧾 Example 2
Input:
["abcd","dcba","lls","s","sssll"]

Output:
[[0,1],[1,0],[3,2],[2,4]]

🧠 INTUITION

We must efficiently check:

For each word:
Split it into:

prefix | suffix


Then check:

1️⃣ If prefix is palindrome → search reversed(suffix)
2️⃣ If suffix is palindrome → search reversed(prefix)

Use a HashMap to store word → index for O(1) lookup.

💡 CORE IDEA

For every word:

Try all possible splits:

"" | word
"w" | "ord"
"wo" | "rd"
"wor" | "d"
"word" | ""


For each split:

Case 1:
If prefix palindrome
→ check reversed(suffix) exists

Case 2:
If suffix palindrome
→ check reversed(prefix) exists

🪜 ALGORITHM

Store word and index in HashMap.

For each word:

For every possible split:

Check prefix palindrome

Check suffix palindrome

Look for reversed counterpart

Store valid pairs.

💻 FULL JAVA CODE
import java.util.*;

public class PalindromePairs {

    public static List<List<Integer>> palindromePairs(String[] words) {

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

        // Step 1: Store words with index
        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
            map.put(words[i], i);
        }

        // Step 2: Check each word
        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {

            String word = words[i];

            for (int j = 0; j <= word.length(); j++) {

                String prefix = word.substring(0, j);
                String suffix = word.substring(j);

                // Case 1: Prefix is palindrome
                if (isPalindrome(prefix)) {

                    String reversedSuffix = new StringBuilder(suffix).reverse().toString();

                    if (map.containsKey(reversedSuffix) &&
                        map.get(reversedSuffix) != i) {

                        result.add(Arrays.asList(map.get(reversedSuffix), i));
                    }
                }

                // Case 2: Suffix is palindrome
                // Avoid duplicate when j == word.length()
                if (j != word.length() && isPalindrome(suffix)) {

                    String reversedPrefix = new StringBuilder(prefix).reverse().toString();

                    if (map.containsKey(reversedPrefix) &&
                        map.get(reversedPrefix) != i) {

                        result.add(Arrays.asList(i, map.get(reversedPrefix)));
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    // Check if string is palindrome
    private static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {

        int left = 0;
        int right = s.length() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right))
                return false;
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String[] words = {"bat","tab","cat"};

        System.out.println(palindromePairs(words));
    }
}

🔍 LINE BY LINE EXPLANATION
🔹 Store words in HashMap
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();


Stores word → index

🔹 Try all splits
for (int j = 0; j <= word.length(); j++)


We allow j == length to include empty suffix.

🔹 Extract prefix & suffix
String prefix = word.substring(0, j);
String suffix = word.substring(j);


Split point at j.

🔹 Case 1: Prefix palindrome

If prefix is palindrome →
We only need reversed suffix in front.

Example:

word = "bat"
prefix = "b"
suffix = "at"

If prefix palindrome:
Need reverse("at") = "ta"

🔹 Check map
map.containsKey(reversedSuffix)


If exists and not same index → valid pair.

🔹 Case 2: Suffix palindrome

Same logic but reversed order.

Important:

j != word.length()


Avoid duplicate cases.

🔹 Palindrome Function

Two pointer approach:

left++
right--


Time: O(L)

⏳ TIME COMPLEXITY

Let:

N = number of words

L = max word length

Total:

O(N × L²)

🧠 INTERVIEW EXPLANATION (Power Version)

"I split each word at every possible position and check two cases: whether the prefix is palindrome or the suffix is palindrome. If so, I search for the reversed complement part in a HashMap to find a valid pair. This reduces the brute force O(N²L) solution to O(NL²)."

